// §7.3.2 accessing X and Z bits of a discrete net in a continuous context. The
// chapter's `converter` example annotates this exact comparison as illegal:
//
//     if (dnet == 1'bx)     // error
//        $display("Error to access x bit in continuous context");
//
// The ORDINARY equality operator is what makes it an error. §7.3.2 lists the
// four features that may carry x and z through the analog context — `===`,
// `!==`, the case/casex/casez statements, and x/z numeric constants — and `==`
// is not among them: it returns x when an operand is x, and "it is an error if
// these operands return x or z bit values when solved". The a2d example one
// page earlier writes the legal form of the same test as `dnet === 1'bx`.
//
// So this is an error in Verilog-AMS HDL as well as outside the Verilog-A
// subset (C.3: "support for x and z values is limited in the analog block to
// the mixed signal context"), and the rejection is conformance in both
// dialects. VerA lands on the literal rather than on the operator — E0130,
// "x/z digit in a number literal" — which is the same verdict reached one step
// earlier; the code is pinned because it is stable, and the clause cite is what
// says which rule the file is about.
//
//! lrm 7.3.2
//! lrm C.3
//! reject E0130
module ch7_xz_ordinary_equality(p, d);
  inout p; electrical p; input d; wire d; real result;
  analog begin
    result = (d == 1'bx) ? 1.0 : 0.0;
    I(p) <+ result;
  end
endmodule
